videos.script:
Close your eyes and try to use your phone. {{eyes}} For some people that is not an exercise; it is every day. They use a screen reader, TalkBack on Android or VoiceOver on the iPhone, and it speaks whatever your app told it. {{reader}}

If the planner's tick button has no label, the screen reader says button, and nothing else. {{label}} If the button is smaller than a fingertip, a student with a tremor misses it. {{target}} If the date is pale grey on white, a student in sunlight cannot read it. {{contrast}}

Today you fix all three, and two more. You write the labels a screen reader reads, side by side on three platforms. You measure the planner's buttons against each platform's minimum size. {{sizes}} You compute contrast with the formula the web's accessibility guidelines publish, and one colour from the last lesson fails. {{formula}} Then text that grows to twice its size, motion that can be turned down, and a done state that does not rest on colour alone. {{colour}}
